---
import GovernanceDocument from '~/components/ui/GovernanceDocument.astro';
import { getPermalink } from '~/utils/permalinks';

const sections = [
  { id: 'standards', title: 'Ethical standards' },
  { id: 'support-and-boundaries', title: 'Peer support and boundaries' },
  { id: 'raising-a-concern', title: 'Raising a concern' },
  { id: 'restorative-options', title: 'Voluntary restorative options' },
  { id: 'independent-review', title: 'Independent review and recusal' },
];
---

<GovernanceDocument
  page="code-of-conduct"
  title="Ethical Code of Conduct"
  category="Governance"
  description="A public overview of ethical expectations, voluntary restorative options, and safeguards for an impartial response to serious concerns."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="standards" aria-labelledby="standards-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="standards-heading">Ethical standards</h2>
    <p>
      This guide draws on HTA’s current working code for affiliated roles, including volunteers, presenters,
      facilitators, vendors, contractors, committee members, and partner representatives in events, support spaces, and
      related online activities. It summarizes the working text; it is not a record of formal adoption. The <a
        href="https://psychedelickc.org/coc/">Psychedelic Society of Kansas City publishes its own Code of Conduct</a
      >
      for that program.
    </p>
    <p>
      Treat people with dignity. Respect their autonomy, culture, and access needs. Be honest about what you know,
      acknowledge uncertainty, and use evidence when offering information. Do not exploit cultural, spiritual, clinical,
      or organizational authority. Disclose conflicts and do not use HTA spaces to pressure people into purchases,
      donations, or private services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="support-and-boundaries" aria-labelledby="support-and-boundaries-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="support-and-boundaries-heading">Peer support and boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Peer support can include listening, grounding, de-escalation, and helping someone find appropriate care. It is
        not diagnosis, treatment, or a chance to solicit clients. Escalate urgent or out-of-scope needs to a lead,
        medical staff, or emergency services as appropriate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ask before touching or recording anyone. Honor refusals, consider whether the person is in a position to
        consent, and never pressure them to share personal details. Do not pursue sexual or romantic contact with
        someone you are supporting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Do not supply, administer, or facilitate access to controlled substances, recruit people for underground
        services, or work while impaired. Keep personal information private and disclose only what is necessary for
        safety or legal obligations.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="raising-a-concern" aria-labelledby="raising-a-concern-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="raising-a-concern-heading">Raising a concern</h2>
    <p>
      Use the <a href={getPermalink('/contact')}>general contact page</a> to ask where to raise a concern. It is not a secure
      case intake channel. Do not send sensitive allegations or another person’s private information there. If there is immediate
      danger, call 911.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concerns should be taken seriously, with attention to immediate safety and retaliation. No one should face
      retaliation for reporting in good faith or participating in a review. Share information only as needed for a fair
      and safe response; absolute confidentiality is not promised.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="restorative-options" aria-labelledby="restorative-options-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="restorative-options-heading">Voluntary restorative options</h2>
    <p>
      A restorative approach can help people name the harm and agree on what repair would look like, when it is safe and
      they freely choose to take part. No one is required to meet with another person or accept mediation. See
      <a href={getPermalink('/war-on-drugs')}>War on Drugs &amp; Restorative Justice</a> for the principles behind this approach.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="independent-review" aria-labelledby="independent-review-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="independent-review-heading">Independent review and recusal</h2>
    <p>
      Serious allegations should not be decided by friends, close colleagues, or people with a stake in the outcome. An
      impartial review requires independence from people involved in the matter and from pressure to reach a particular
      result. It is separate from voluntary restorative work.
    </p>
    <p>
      For serious allegations, the non-recused board members choose a pool of eligible reviewers. A case-specific
      committee forms independently from that pool. Any board member or volunteer with a personal connection to anyone
      involved must step aside. That means taking no part in choosing reviewers, reviewing evidence, deliberating, or
      making a decision on the case.
    </p>
    <p>
      A fair review gives people an opportunity to respond where safe. Depending on what is needed, responses can range
      from coaching or training to restrictions, removal, or referral when required. Interim protections can be
      necessary before findings are made. Restorative options do not replace these protections or anyone’s ability to
      seek emergency or outside help.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href={getPermalink('/bylaws')}>bylaws overview</a> explains the distinction between organizational governance
      and expectations for conduct.
    </p>
  </section>
</GovernanceDocument>
